ral","person_id":"tvfy_10131","person_slug":"richard-colbeck","vote":"no"},{"name":"Robert Ray","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10531","person_slug":"robert-ray","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Rod Kemp","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10361","person_slug":"rod-kemp","vote":"no"},{"name":"Ron Boswell","party":"Nationals","person_id":"tvfy_10057","person_slug":"ron-boswell","vote":"no"},{"name":"Ross Lightfoot","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10389","person_slug":"ross-lightfoot","vote":"no"},{"name":"Russell Trood","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10640","person_slug":"russell-trood","vote":"no"},{"name":"Ruth Webber","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10662","person_slug":"ruth-webber","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Sandy Macdonald","party":"Nationals","person_id":"tvfy_10402","person_slug":"sandy-macdonald","vote":"no"},{"name":"Santo Santoro","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10558","person_slug":"santo-santoro","vote":"no"},{"name":"Stephen Conroy","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10142","person_slug":"stephen-conroy","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Stephen Parry","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10507","person_slug":"stephen-parry","vote":"no"},{"name":"Steve Hutchins","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10323","person_slug":"steve-hutchins","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Ursula Stephens","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10609","person_slug":"ursula-stephens","vote":"aye"}],"name":"Declaration of Percentage of Commonwealth Supported Places — Motion for Disallowance","noes":32,"question":"Penny Wong I move: That the Declaration of percentage of Commonwealth supported places to be provided by Table A providers for a course of study in medicine, made under paragraph 36-35(1)(b) of the Higher Education Support Act 2003 , be disallowed. I am moving this motion on behalf of the opposition to disallow the Minister for Education, Science and Training’s declaration increasing the cap to 25 per cent for the number of full fee paying domestic undergraduate medical students able to be enrolled in Australia’s public universities. This is a fundamental point of difference between the government and the Labor opposition. This government wants to allow yet more $200,000 degrees, whereas Labor believes in fair and equitable entry into our universities based on merit, not on money. We believe in entry to universities based on merit, not on the size of your bank balance. The government’s only solution to the doctors shortage is to allow more students to jump the queue and buy their way into a medical course, even if those students with money have lower marks. Labor believes increasing HECS funded medical places is the only way to ensure more doctors are willing to work in rural and regional areas. The policy change that this regulation encompasses is the result of an agreement announced by the Prime Minister following the Council of Australian Governments meeting in February. There is no doubt that this was hastily cobbled together and not thought through. We learnt during a recent Senate estimates hearing that the Department of Education, Science and Training—and presumably the education minister—did not know about the change until the public announcement was actually made. And it shows. Fumbling this announcement, the Prime Minister repeatedly demonstrated his complete ignorance and lack of understanding about how Australia’s university system works. During the COAG press conference, the Prime Minister said: ... there would be an increase from 10 to 25 percent in th","result":"negative","slug":"division-federal-senate-2761","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2006-03-29/5"}
